---
import Plain from "../layouts/Plain.astro";
import { site } from "../lib/config";

const pictureNames = {
  apod: "NASA Astronomy Picture of the Day",
  met: "The Metropolitan Museum of Art",
  wikipedia: "Wikipedia’s picture of the day",
  aic: "Art Institute of Chicago",
  rijks: "Rijksmuseum",
};
---

<Plain
	title={`About · ${site.site.title}`}
	description={`About ${site.site.title}: sources, privacy, accessibility and code.`}
>
	<Fragment slot="title">About this page</Fragment>

	<p>
		A daily page for {site.location.name}: pictures, facts, weather, a word, a quiz, games and recipes{site.content.events ? ", plus what's on nearby" : ""}. A new paper starts at midnight, local time.
	</p>

	<p>
		Games open on their own sites in a new tab. Opening a game marks it as played here; the page can't see
		whether you finish it. Your marks, streaks and quiz answers stay in this browser, so another browser
		or device has its own progress.
	</p>

	<h2>Accessibility</h2>
	<p>
		The aim is WCAG 2.2 AA: readable contrast and visible keyboard focus.
		The page respects your reduced-motion setting and reads in full without JavaScript.
		If something gets in your way,{" "}
		<a href="https://github.com/simonvanlierde/duinlab-today/issues">open an issue</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Sources</h2>
	<p>Each item on the page links to where it came from.</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Facts, the featured article and on this day: Wikipedia. The word: Wiktionary.</li>
		{site.content.pictures.length > 0 && <li>Pictures: {site.content.pictures.map((kind) => pictureNames[kind]).join(", ")}.</li>}
		<li>The quiz: Open Trivia DB (CC BY-SA 4.0). The quote: ZenQuotes.</li>
		<li>
			The weather: <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a>. The rain line comes from{" "}
			{site.content.radar === "knmi" ? "KNMI’s radar when fresh, falling back to Buienradar or Open-Meteo." : site.content.radar === "buienradar" ? "Buienradar when available, falling back to Open-Meteo." : "Open-Meteo’s forecast."}{" "}
			The weather line opens the week ahead on{" "}
			<a href={`https://www.meteoblue.com/en/weather/week/${site.location.latitude}N${site.location.longitude}E`}>meteoblue</a>.
		</li>
		<li>
			Air quality and pollen: Open-Meteo, shown only when the European AQI is 60 or more or a pollen count
			is high by MeteoSwiss's classes.
		</li>
		{site.content.tides && <li>Tide times at Scheveningen: <a href="https://waterinfo.rws.nl/">Rijkswaterstaat</a>.</li>}
		{site.content.sea && <li>Sea temperature and waves: <a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/marine-weather-api">Open-Meteo's marine API</a>.</li>}
		{site.content.events && <li>
			This week's shows and events: <a href="https://www.paard.nl/">Paard</a>,{" "}
			<a href="https://www.amare.nl/">Amare</a> and <a href="https://denhaag.com/en/agenda">DenHaag.com</a>.
		</li>}
		<li>Recipes: the newest from the Guardian, BBC Good Food and Love and Lemons.</li>
		<li>
			The planets, full moons, solstices, equinoxes and eclipses:{" "}
			<a href="https://github.com/cosinekitty/astronomy">Astronomy Engine</a> (MIT). The meteor shower peaks
			are fixed approximate dates.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Your browser works out the sky from the sun's position over {site.location.name}.</p>

	<h2>Privacy</h2>
	<p>
		No cookies or analytics. Your game marks, streaks and quiz answers stay in this browser.
		Clear this site's data in your browser settings to remove them.
	</p>
	<p>
		A Cloudflare Worker serves the page. Cloudflare keeps request logs for a few days: the address asked for,
		the time, and connection details such as your IP address. I only read them to trace a fault. The day's pictures load straight from NASA, The Met, Wikimedia, the Art Institute, the Rijksmuseum or YouTube, so
		they see your IP address too.
	</p>
	<p>
		The <a href="https://github.com/simonvanlierde/duinlab-today/blob/main/docs/privacy.md">full privacy statement</a>
		says what you can ask and how to reach me.
	</p>

	<h2>Code</h2>
	<p>
		<a href="https://github.com/simonvanlierde/duinlab-today">Code on GitHub</a> (MIT licence).
		The day's data is at <a href="/today.json">today.json</a>.
	</p>
</Plain>
